# coord board-doctor FAST sweep: the deterministic mechanical repairs
# only (`coord drive-queue sweep --mechanical-only`), on a tight cadence —
# split off from `coord-board-doctor.timer`'s hourly full sweep.
#
# The hourly sweep's own rationale ("no correctness reason to run it
# tighter") only ever covered the expensive half: walking both queues
# and classifying every cause with `gh`, then — if a repo opted in — a
# fix-it leg for an ambiguous cause. That reasoning never applied to the
# mechanical half: a handful of pre-reviewed, side-effect-safe queue
# repairs (a PR merged out-of-band, a stale HUMAN_REQUIRED latch, a
# PENDING merge-queue row that already reads green/approved/mergeable
# but the ordinary merge tick keeps missing, a conflict-fix that
# completed clean but never moved the branch, a frozen blocked/parked
# row whose block cause already resolved) — exactly as safe to run every
# minute or two as `coord-serve`'s own `merge.auto_drain` tick. Left on
# the hourly-only cadence, one of these can sit broken for up to an
# hour even though the fix is a plain `coord merge --only` (or
# equivalent) away the whole time.
#
# `--mechanical-only` (coord/commands/drive_queue.py) makes this
# genuinely a DIFFERENT, narrower invocation, not just a faster repeat
# of the same one: it drops every row outside
# `coord.board_doctor_policy.mechanical_causes()` before classification,
# so this timer never dispatches a fix-it leg, never escalates a
# non-mechanical cause, and never touches that row's own convergence
# counter. The investigate/alert tiers keep their current hourly budget
# and per-repo opt-in entirely via `coord-board-doctor.timer`, unaffected
# by this one running underneath it.
#
# Install: see deploy/coord-board-doctor-fast.service's header.

[Unit]
Description=Run coord board-doctor's mechanical-only sweep on a tight cadence

[Timer]
OnBootSec=2min
OnUnitActiveSec=1min
AccuracySec=10s
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
